### Where TinaCMS is better

- **Editing on the page.** TinaCMS has visual click-to-edit, and it works with Astro. Mica has forms only.
- **A hosted service.** TinaCloud runs the backend for you. Mica has no hosted offering yet. A Mica Cloud is planned.
- **Review.** The Editorial Workflow (Business plan and up) puts edits on a branch with a draft pull request. Mica has no review step in the editor.
- **Search and MDX.** TinaCMS offers search through its index on TinaCloud, and it supports MDX components. Mica uses Markdoc.
- **Activity.** TinaCMS is maintained by SSW and is very active.

### Where Mica is better

- **Agents.** Mica has an MCP server now, built into the free editor. It runs locally (stdio and HTTP) and in the self-hosted Mica, where each person signs in and allows each agent. An agent reads the content model, and Mica checks every value it sends. Every save carries the revision the agent read, so an agent never overwrites a newer save. Agents save drafts and publish only when allowed. TinaCMS lists an MCP server as Coming Soon.
- **No database and no index.** TinaCMS keeps your content in a Data Layer. TinaCloud hosts it, or you run MongoDB or Redis/Vercel KV. A self-hosted TinaCMS indexes at build time. Mica reads files and needs no database.
- **Many large files.** Many large photos and files make a Git repository heavy and slow to clone. Mica keeps them in Git LFS, so the history stays small, in the local and the self-hosted editor. TinaCMS does not support Git LFS (the documentation names limits of the GitHub API).
- **External data.** Your entries can link to data that lives elsewhere: product data, place names, regions or codes. Mica uses thousands of items from a JSON or CSV file of your website, and never edits them. The TinaCMS documentation does not describe this.
- **Git hosts.** TinaCMS works with GitHub only. Mica works with any Git host.
- **Self-hosting.** Self-hosted TinaCMS needs a backend function, a database adapter, a Git provider and an auth provider. It lacks search, Git-backed media and branch switching. Mica self-hosted is one container with a volume, HTTPS in front, an OpenID Connect client and a Git token. The TinaCMS documentation does not describe OIDC sign-in.
- **Astro.** Mica content reaches Astro as Content Collections with types, and there is no generated client.
- **Price per user.** TinaCloud free allows 2 users, and its plans are priced by project and users. Mica has no paid plan and no user limit.
- **Large collections.** TinaCMS says that reference fields can time out on large collections. Mica is tested with 1,000 entries in a collection.
- **Map.** TinaCMS has no map field. Mica has one, and it stores the zoom with the place, so the page can show the same view.

### Equal, or not documented

- Both use a TypeScript model and have an official Astro package.
- Both save as files in Git, and neither has a batch publish.
- Content in a Git submodule: the TinaCMS documentation does not say.
- Upload limits: TinaCMS rejects .html, .js and .xml files and does not document GPX or ZIP. Mica checks JPEG, PNG, GIF, WebP, AVIF, PDF, GPX, KML, GeoJSON, ZIP and CSV files by their bytes.
